% \paragraph{Safe dimension recognition.}
% The column-gap setters must reject malformed input before TeX attempts a
% dimension assignment, because an unrestricted assignment can consume later
% document tokens while searching for a unit. The validator accepts three
% forms. The first is a complete explicit dimension in a standard TeX unit,
% with optional spaces before the unit and an optional |true| prefix on the
% physical units. The second is one existing dimension or skip register. The
% third is one token whose meaning is a dimension or glue parameter of TeX,
% such as |\parindent| or |\baselineskip|. Those parameters are primitives
% rather than registers, so the register tests do not recognize them. The
% constant lists the meaning of all 21 dimension parameters and all 15 glue
% parameters of TeX, and the membership test compares the meaning of the
% candidate token, which also accepts a |\let| copy of a parameter. The
% three math glue parameters are excluded because they cannot be assigned
% to a length. The validator does not evaluate arbitrary macros or
% dimension expressions at this boundary. The input is expanded through
% |\text_expand:n|, so a macro that expands to a dimension is accepted while
% a robust command is left intact and rejected. The two LaTeX2e-facing
% wrappers translate the conditional and the named message for the
% configuration layer.
